        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"zuccoli-lamore-di-loredana-fc8a54e562ac9eac\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The highlighted “ti amo” is embedded in Filippo’s direct avowal “sento che ti amo” (“I feel that I love you”). It strongly presents his love as a true emotional state. In context it also contributes to a proposal of elopement and a relational undertaking, giving moderate P support, though the clause itself is primarily an avowal. Nothing indicates that the words escape as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The main uncertainty is the degree of P: Filippo’s surrounding proposal and offer of his life make the avowal commitment-bearing, but “sento che ti amo” itself remains principally truth-conditional.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Filippo speaks directly to Loredana in represented dialogue; the highlighted love proposition is grammatically embedded under “sento che” (“I feel that”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Filippo has just reconceived their friendship as romantic love and asks Loredana to flee Venice with him. His repeated epistemic and experiential framing—“So che ti amo, sento che ti amo”—presents a conscious realization and avowal, not a verbal reflex. His subsequent offer of “my love and my life,” request for acceptance, and plan to leave immediately give the avowal some undertaking force, despite his explicit refusal or inability to marry her because of family opposition.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“So che ti amo, sento che ti amo, sento che noi possiamo essere felici.”\",\"supports\":\"The verbs “know” and “feel” explicitly frame the highlighted clause as an avowal of a loving state, strongly supporting T and weighing against reflex-like E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Ma ti offro lo stesso il mio amore e la mia vita.... Dimmi che accetti ... Partiremo subito.”\",\"supports\":\"The avowal occurs within an offer of love and life and a request that Loredana accept an immediate shared course of action, providing contextual support for P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Filippo first says that what they call friendship “non è che amore” and that he has realized this himself.\",\"supports\":\"This reflective discovery supports a considered assertion of love rather than an expressive or reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative present-tense Italian explicit love expression, with exact match “ti amo.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted span is the explicit first-person love predication being classified.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the explicit avowal, while P captures its secondary role within Filippo’s proposed relational undertaking. Delivery and emotion do not require E, and no important core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
